<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alain LeRoy Locke</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1885">1885</a>–<a href="/years/1954">1954</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Alain Locke, Arthur Leroy Locke</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alain LeRoy Locke was an American writer, philosopher and educator, known as the philosophical architect, or Dean, of the Harlem <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> in 1885, he graduated from Harvard in 1907 and that year became the first African-American <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> Scholar; at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> several colleges refused him before Hertford College admitted him. He took his PhD in philosophy at Harvard in 1918 and chaired the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of philosophy at Howard University, where he taught the first classes on race relations. In 1925 he expanded a special issue of Survey Graphic into The New Negro, a collection of writings by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-americans">African Americans</a> that became a landmark in Black literature.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alain-leroy-locke.jpg" alt="Alain LeRoy Locke" width="330" height="448" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alain_LeRoy_Locke_cr.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
